---
/**
 * A cue in a scene lesson's text (docs/specs/2026-11-20-visual-lessons-design.md, §3.1): from here
 * on, scene `id` of the lesson's module (src/scenes/lessons/<translationKey>.ts) plays. It goes on
 * its own line, before the heading of the part it illustrates, with the same id in both languages.
 * `<Scene end />` closes the scenes: the text after it has none.
 * It draws nothing and takes no line number (`hidden`): the engine measures the element after it.
 *
 * It also brings the engine's CSS: only the lessons that use it (they all have cues) get it, in their
 * <head>, because Astro passes a component's CSS on to the pages whose MDX uses the component. Imported
 * by ScenePanel instead, it would load on every page of the site, which all render TwoColumnContent.
 * Each world's CSS reaches the page the same way, from the MDX: it imports its lesson's
 * src/scenes/lessons/<key>.css, which imports only the worlds that lesson draws (scenes.test.ts).
 */
import '~/scenes/core/scenes.css';

type Props = { id: string; end?: never } | { end: true; id?: never };

const { id, end } = Astro.props;
---

{end ? (
  <span class="scene-cue" data-scene-end hidden />
) : (
  <span class="scene-cue" data-scene={id} hidden />
)}
